==Administration==
{{main|Toyozawa Metropolitan Government}}
The Toyozawa Metropolitan Government administers the entire metropolis as a unified entity, functioning as both a city and a prefecture. The executive branch is headed by a directly elected Governor, currently [[Ikeda Ayato]], an independent. The legislative branch is the Toyozawa Metropolitan Assembly.

===Municipalities===
Toyozawa Metropolis is administratively organized into [[Special wards of Toyozawa|12 special wards]] (特別区, ''tokubetsu-ku'') in the urban core, alongside surrounding suburban cities and municipal districts. The special wards encompass [[Kanda-ku|Kanda]] (population 802,808), the commercial and microelectronics design centre; [[Toyozawa Chuo-ku|Toyozawa Chūō]] (population 452,332), the central civic, banking, and railway crossroads; [[Kadoma-ku|Kadoma]] (population 297,597), hosting major semiconductor fabrication complexes; [[Neyagawa-ku|Neyagawa]] (population 230,459); [[Abeno-ku|Abeno]] (population 195,687); [[Miyakojima-ku|Miyakojima]] (population 165,334); [[Ikuno-ku|Ikuno]] (population 160,221); [[Suminoe-ku|Suminoe]] (population 122,459), the centre of heavy shipbuilding and steel fabrication; [[Tondabayashi-ku|Tondabayashi]] (population 120,412); [[Habikino-ku|Habikino]] (population 112,456); and [[Nishinari-ku|Nishinari]] (population 110,432). Beyond the special wards, the metropolitan territory encompasses adjacent satellite municipalities including the industrial cities of [[Neyagawa]] and [[Katano]], as well as the pivotal eastern demographic hinge city of [[Isshinji]] (population 802,808).

==Economy==
{{Main|Economy of Toyozawa}}
Toyozawa is the industrial and technological engine of Okaiken—the place where the nation's industrial output, semiconductor technology, and AI infrastructure are forged. While [[Okami]] serves as the administrative and financial heart of the nation, Toyozawa drives Okaiken's standing in the world economy through its concentration of advanced manufacturing, semiconductor fabrication, and artificial intelligence infrastructure.

The city's nickname {{Nihongo|''The Forge''|鍛冶場|Kajiba}} reflects this industrial identity. Toyozawa's economy is built on four pillars: semiconductor and AI hardware manufacturing, data centre infrastructure, shipbuilding and heavy industry, and precision manufacturing.

===Semiconductors and AI hardware===
[[File:Kizan Systems Toyozawa Fab.png|thumb|right|250px|The Kizan Systems Toyozawa Fabrication Plant, the nation's primary facility for tensor processing unit production.]]

Toyozawa is home to [[Kizan Systems]], the nation's premier semiconductor and tensor processing unit (TPU) manufacturer and the primary global competitor to foreign GPU manufacturers. Following the [[Digital Infrastructure Act]] of 2008, the government subsidised the creation of Kizan Systems to reduce Okaiken's dependence on foreign chip suppliers, and the company has since become a global leader in AI accelerator hardware.

The [[Kizan Systems Toyozawa Fabrication Plant]], located in the [[Kadoma]] ward, is the nation's largest semiconductor fabrication facility, producing tensor processing units used for deep learning, neural network training, and AI inference. The plant employs approximately 12,400 workers and generates annual revenues exceeding 円120 billion. Kizan's ''Tensei'' series of TPAs (tensor processing accelerators) are used in data centres throughout the East Fosian Sphere and are the hardware foundation for the [[Satori AI]] ecosystem.

===The Toyozawa Data Corridor===
The {{Nihongo|'''Toyozawa Data Corridor'''|豊沢データ回廊|Toyozawa dēta-kairō}} is a 15-kilometre strip of data centre facilities extending from the [[Jurakutei]] artificial island to the [[Neyagawa]] ward. The Corridor is the densest concentration of data centres in the East Fosian Sphere, housing over 180 facilities with a combined IT load capacity of approximately 2.4 gigawatts.

The Corridor's development was driven by three factors: the cool volcanic highlands of northern Yagikoshu, where high-altitude plateaus provide natural cooling for server infrastructure; the reliable power supply from the nearby [[Suzuka Geothermal Complex]] and the [[Yagikoshu Nuclear Power Station]]; and the proximity to the [[Toyozawa Central Station]] hub, which provides high-speed connectivity to all three Shinkansen lines.

Major enterprise operators across the Corridor include [[Kizan Cloud Services]], providing cloud computing and tensor-driven AI inference; [[Toyozawa Data Holdings]], managing wholesale colocation and enterprise server facilities; [[Satori AI Infrastructure]], hosting national foundation model training clusters; and [[Shendan Networks]], managing international subsea cable landings, carrier-neutral telecommunications exchanges, and edge content distribution networks.

===Shipbuilding and heavy industry===
The [[Sangoku Heavy Industries]] Toyozawa shipyard, located in the [[Konohana]] ward, is one of the nation's three primary shipbuilding complexes alongside the [[Aizawa]] shipyards on Ishijima and the [[Seto]] shipyards on the Katsuran Strait. The Toyozawa shipyard specialises in LNG carriers, ice-class vessels for northern routes, and autonomous cargo ships. The facility employs approximately 8,200 workers and has an annual production capacity of 18 large vessels.

The [[Toyozawa Industrial Zone]] in the [[Suminoe]] ward houses steel processing facilities, chemical plants, and heavy machinery manufacturers. The zone is connected to the Port of Toyozawa by dedicated rail lines and conveyor systems, enabling efficient export of manufactured goods.

===Precision manufacturing and electronics===
Toyozawa's precision manufacturing sector produces machine tools, industrial robots, and medical devices. The city is also a centre for consumer electronics, with several major manufacturers maintaining production facilities in the metropolitan area.

===Historical commercial role===
Historically, Toyozawa developed as the center of Okaiken's rice trade. The [[Okami Rice Exchange]], though headquartered in the capital, relied on Toyozawa's warehouses and distribution networks. This commercial tradition continues today, with the city serving as a major logistics hub for the region.

===Transportation and tourism===
The transportation sector is a major employer, with Toyozawa Central Station and the associated rail infrastructure supporting thousands of jobs. The tourism industry has also grown significantly, centered on the city's historical sites—including [[Shirakawa Memorial Park]], the site of the former Toyozawa Castle where [[Emperor Shirakawa]] died in the [[Kinzō Incident]] of 1625—and cultural attractions.

==Transportation==
{{main|Transportation in Toyozawa}}
Toyozawa is the primary transportation hub of Okaiken, serving as the central node of the nation's high-speed rail network.

===Rail===
[[File:Toyozawa Central Station.png|thumb|right|250px|Toyozawa Central Station, the primary hub of the Okaiken Shinkansen network where all three high-speed lines converge.]]
[[Toyozawa Central Station]] (豊沢中央駅, Toyozawa Chūō-eki) serves as the grand convergence hub of the national [[Okaiken Shinkansen]] network, where all three high-speed trunk lines meet. The [[Harujima Shinkansen]] connects Toyozawa northward to the capital of [[Okami]] in 2 hours 31 minutes via flagship ''Nozomi'' express runs and onward to [[Shinagawa]] in 4 hours 35 minutes. Crossing undersea via the Katsuran Strait, the [[Ishijima Shinkansen]] links Toyozawa with [[Sandaguchi Station (Okaiken)|Sandaguchi]] in [[Sanda, Seto|Sanda]], [[Minatojima]], and the industrial metropolis of [[Aizawa]] in 3 hours 15 minutes. Heading southeast through the Shimamoto gap, the [[Minami Yagikoshu Shinkansen]] links Toyozawa to [[Mito Station (Okaiken)|Mito]] in fourteen minutes before continuing south to [[Kii]]. Beyond the elevated platforms, the station incorporates an advanced Y-junction flying junction design that facilitates split and combine operations, allowing 16-car ''Nozomi'' services arriving from Okami to decouple on the platform, sending the lead eight cars toward Aizawa and the trailing eight cars toward Kii without passenger transfers.

===Urban rail and private railway kingdom===
{{main|Toyozawa Rapid Transit|Toyokyu|Mitoyo Electric Railway|Transport in Okaiken}}
Toyozawa possesses one of the world's densest and most competitive urban rail ecosystems, often described as the "private railway kingdom" (私鉄王国, ''shitetsu ōkoku'') on an unprecedented scale. Commuter mobility is organized around two colossal terminal poles linked by grade-separated rapid transit. In the northern core, Toyozawa Station (Kita) integrates the ground-level concourses and elevated Shinkansen viaducts of [[OR Yagikoshu]] (Okaiken Railways), the elevated 9-platform, 10-track terminal of [[Toyokyu]] on the third floor of the flagship twelve-story Toyokyu Department Store, and the multi-level subterranean terminal of the [[Toyo-Hansoku Railway]] (Toyo-Han), directly connected to the extensive "Whity Toyozawa" underground shopping city. Three kilometres to the south, Minami-Toyo Station acts as the metropolis's southern gateway, anchored by the grand European-style vaulted headhouse of the [[Nantoyo Electric Railway]] and the southern interurban platforms of the [[Mitoyo Electric Railway]], forming a bustling commercial counter-pole to the northern terminal.

Connecting Toyozawa Central directly to [[Mito Station (Okaiken)|Mito]], the heavily contested "Great Corridor" intercity raceway features three parallel multi-track alignments running through the Shimamoto gap along the river basin. On the northern hillside terrace, the prestigious [[Toyokyu]] (Toyozawa Kyuko Railway / 豊急) operates its quad-track Mito Main Line through affluent residential communities. Celebrated for its patrician elegance, lustrous dark maroon enamel trainsets, mahogany wood-grain interiors, golden-olive plush velour seating, and classical orchestral departure fanfares, Toyokyu operates four core radial routes including the Mito Main Line ("Golden Route" departing every eight minutes), the Mie Line paralleling the Harujima Shinkansen, the scenic Ise Line with luxury limited express trainsets, and commuter branches to Kurosaki and Osaki. Along the southern riverbank and historic merchant quarters, the rival [[Mitoyo Electric Railway]] (水豊電気鉄道, ''Mitoyo'') runs its double-track Ichijoudani Line (一条谷線) and the Mito–Mie corridor, renowned for its bi-level double-decker commuter carriages and dedicated "Premium Car" reserved-seating service on rapid express trains. Running down the center on a continuous elevated viaduct, the national railway [[OR Yagikoshu]] operates its 4-track Cape-gauge OR Yagikoshu Main Line, running 130 km/h Special Rapid (''Shin-Kaisoku'') services in a 24-minute downtown sprint.

Specialized private rail operators expand connectivity across adjacent topographic and social corridors. Running literally 500 metres south of the northern mainline through dense factory wards, docklands, and baseball stadiums is the [[Toyo-Hansoku Railway]] (豊阪電鉄, ''Toyo-Han''), revered as the "working-class hero" of Toyozawa. Toyo-Han fields iconic "Jet Cars" (ジェットカー) with extreme 4.5 km/h/s acceleration rates to service closely spaced factory-district stops without sacrificing schedule velocity. Operating from Minami-Toyo Station as the "Gate to the South and Sea," the [[Nantoyo Electric Railway]] (南豊電気鉄道, ''Nantoyo'') runs the high-speed Airport Line to [[Shirakawa Tenno International Airport]] with retro-futuristic aerodynamic express trainsets featuring circular aircraft-style portal windows, as well as the southern Mountain Line to sacred pilgrimage ranges. Mountain commuter access to the north is provided by the [[Sankuro Railway]] (三黒鉄道, ''Sankuro''), which scales steep grades across Mount Hoshisasa to connect Toyozawa and Kurosaki through the high-density basin of [[Sanda, Seto|Sanda]], with an auxiliary feeder line connecting Mito to Sanda. Along the coast of the Katsuran Sea, the [[Osaki Railway]] (大崎鉄道) runs parallel to the Ishijima Shinkansen, linking [[Osaki, Seto|Osaki]] in Seto Prefecture with western Toyozawa.

Grade-separated urban circulation is provided by [[Toyozawa Rapid Transit]] (豊沢高速鉄道), operating 6 subway lines across 115.8 kilometres and 94 stations, carrying over 3.12 million daily passengers. The crimson Chūō Line acts as the indispensable north-south transit spine connecting Toyozawa Kita to Minami-Toyo, while extensive reciprocal through-running agreements allow private railway trains from Mitoyo, Sankuro, and Toyo-Han to operate directly into subway tunnels. Due to the extreme 14-metre tidal swings of Toyozawa Bay, all coastal station portals are equipped with automated pneumatic floodgates capable of sealing station portals within 45 seconds of a tidal surge warning.

===Air===
[[Shirakawa Tenno International Airport]] (白川天皇国際空港, Shirakawa Tennō Kokusai Kūkō), located in the western suburbs, serves as the primary airport for the Toyozawa metropolitan area and the second-busiest international gateway in Okaiken after [[Okami-Tobu International Airport]].

===Ferry===
Toyozawa's port facilities handle both passenger and cargo traffic. Ferry services connect Toyozawa with other coastal cities and the outlying islands. The port processed over 8.9 million passengers in 2023, making it the second-busiest ferry port in Okaiken.

==Education==
Toyozawa is a major educational center, hosting several universities and research institutions. The city's historical role as an intellectual center during the Honda period established a tradition of learning that continues today.

Major institutions include Toyozawa Metropolitan University, established in 1921, and several private universities specializing in commerce, engineering, and the arts. The city also hosts numerous specialized research institutes, particularly in the fields of logistics and transportation technology.

==Culture and landmarks==
===Shirakawa Memorial Park===
{{main|Shirakawa Memorial Park}}
[[File:Shirakawa Memorial Park.png|thumb|left|250px|Shirakawa Memorial Park, built on the site of the former Toyozawa Castle.]]

The {{Nihongo|Shirakawa Memorial Park|白川記念公園|Shirakawa Kinen Kōen}} occupies the site of the former Toyozawa Castle, destroyed in the Kinzō Incident of 1625. The park features a reconstructed portion of the castle, including the main keep which houses the Shirakawa Memorial Museum. The museum displays artifacts from the Dual Sovereignty period and exhibits on the life of Emperor Shirakawa.

The park also contains the {{Nihongo|Bridge of Heaven Memorial|天の橋記念碑|Ten no Hashi Kinenhi}}, marking the site where Emperor Shirakawa made his last stand against the Ii forces. The memorial is a popular pilgrimage site for those honoring the emperor's memory.

===Temples and shrines===
Toyozawa's nickname ''City of Temples'' reflects its high concentration of historic religious sites. The {{Nihongo|Seien-ji temple|清円寺}}, founded in the 14th century, houses important Buddhist artworks and serves as the headquarters of an influential sect. The {{Nihongo|Kyudo-jinja shrine|弓道神社}} is dedicated to the martial art of archery and hosts annual competitions drawing participants from across Okaiken.

===Cuisine===
As the historic ''Nation's Kitchen,'' Toyozawa has a rich culinary tradition. The city is known for {{Nihongo|''aka-funi soba''|赤府蕎麦}}, a noodle dish featuring a rich, savory broth, and numerous regional specialties developed to feed the merchant classes during the Honda period. The city's historic markets continue to operate, offering fresh seafood from Toyozawa Bay and produce from the surrounding agricultural regions.

==Notable people==
Prominent historical and cultural figures associated with Toyozawa include [[Honda Shigetada]] (1562–1625), the founder of the [[Honda Shogunate]] and later [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]], who constructed Toyozawa Castle and established the city's merchant privileges; [[Hayashi Tadamasa]] (1570–1645), the master civil engineer who planned the city's intertidal canal network and stone flood levees; [[Matsui Yuki]] (1899–1972), the pioneering industrialist who financed the Haramaki New City reclamation and heavy machinery districts; and [[Kobayashi Kenji]] (born 1952), the architect responsible for the historic reconstruction of the Toyozawa Castle keep and Shirakawa Memorial Hall.

